1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,059
The night of February 13th, 1951.

2
00:00:03,079 --> 00:00:07,009
Chipyong-ni, Yangpyeong, Gyeonggi Province.

3
00:00:07,178 --> 00:00:09,570
Inside a small snow-covered basin,

4
00:00:09,590 --> 00:00:11,301
fifty-six hundred American and

5
00:00:11,321 --> 00:00:13,088
French soldiers were trapped.

6
00:00:13,272 --> 00:00:15,158
Chinese troops filled every ridge

7
00:00:15,178 --> 00:00:16,569
around them, and every road

8
00:00:16,589 --> 00:00:18,662
out behind them had been cut.

9
00:00:18,722 --> 00:00:21,343
Chipyong-ni had become a small island

10
00:00:21,363 --> 00:00:23,330
in the middle of an enemy sea.

11
00:00:23,525 --> 00:00:25,445
Chinese intelligence reportedly

12
00:00:25,465 --> 00:00:27,266
estimated the garrison holding this

13
00:00:27,286 --> 00:00:29,542
village at barely a thousand men.

14
00:00:29,671 --> 00:00:32,087
A fight that would be over in a single

15
00:00:32,107 --> 00:00:34,658
night. That is what they believed.

16
00:00:34,748 --> 00:00:37,783
But in reality, more than five times

17
00:00:37,803 --> 00:00:40,092
that number were dug in inside.

18
00:00:40,276 --> 00:00:42,599
But inside the ring stood the 23rd

19
00:00:42,619 --> 00:00:44,397
Infantry Regiment of the U.S.

20
00:00:44,417 --> 00:00:46,890
2nd Division, and the French Battalion.

21
00:00:47,015 --> 00:00:48,543
And surrounding that ring,

22
00:00:48,563 --> 00:00:50,489
according to American records,

23
00:00:50,509 --> 00:00:53,906
were elements of five Chinese divisions.

24
00:00:54,244 --> 00:00:56,296
Three days and three nights of savage

25
00:00:56,316 --> 00:00:59,505
close combat, with bayonets and grenades.

26
00:00:59,621 --> 00:01:01,626
Yet three days later, it was the

27
00:01:01,646 --> 00:01:04,491
Chinese who pulled back first.

28
00:01:04,671 --> 00:01:06,572
The human-wave tactic had not once

29
00:01:06,592 --> 00:01:08,528
been stopped head-on in the three

30
00:01:08,548 --> 00:01:10,987
months since China entered the war.

31
00:01:11,097 --> 00:01:13,558
So why did it grind to a halt, for the first

32
00:01:13,578 --> 00:01:16,730
time, in front of this one small ring?

33
00:01:16,924 --> 00:01:18,783
The Battle of Chipyong-ni.

34
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:20,725
This is the story of the men who,

35
00:01:20,745 --> 00:01:22,591
instead of breaking out and running,

36
00:01:22,611 --> 00:01:25,182
dug in where they stood and held.

37
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:28,508
In October 1950, Chinese forces crossed

38
00:01:28,528 --> 00:01:30,920
the Yalu River and entered the war.

39
00:01:31,058 --> 00:01:32,790
Badly battered at the Chongchon

40
00:01:32,810 --> 00:01:34,817
River and the Chosin Reservoir,

41
00:01:34,837 --> 00:01:37,823
UN forces began to be driven south.

42
00:01:38,018 --> 00:01:41,427
On January 4th, 1951, Seoul fell

43
00:01:41,447 --> 00:01:43,726
into enemy hands once again.

44
00:01:43,814 --> 00:01:46,306
The January Fourth Retreat. Columns

45
00:01:46,326 --> 00:01:48,480
of refugees crossed the frozen Han

46
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:51,388
River and stretched endlessly south.

47
00:01:51,589 --> 00:01:53,728
The Chinese moved only at night,

48
00:01:53,748 --> 00:01:55,549
slipping around through the mountains

49
00:01:55,569 --> 00:01:57,768
to cut off the roads behind.

50
00:01:57,874 --> 00:02:00,267
Among the soldiers spread a dread that

51
00:02:00,287 --> 00:02:03,064
the Chinese simply could not be beaten.

52
00:02:03,251 --> 00:02:04,509
It was at this moment that

53
00:02:04,529 --> 00:02:06,478
Lieutenant General Matthew Ridgway

54
00:02:06,498 --> 00:02:08,629
took over the U.S. Eighth Army.

55
00:02:08,705 --> 00:02:11,371
He arrived at the end of December 1950,

56
00:02:11,391 --> 00:02:13,704
after his predecessor, General Walker,

57
00:02:13,724 --> 00:02:15,703
was killed in a jeep accident.

58
00:02:16,026 --> 00:02:18,305
Ridgway believed that destroying enemy

59
00:02:18,325 --> 00:02:20,889
strength mattered more than holding ground.

60
00:02:21,057 --> 00:02:23,586
From an army that had only known retreat,

61
00:02:23,606 --> 00:02:26,629
he demanded that it learn to fight again.

62
00:02:26,831 --> 00:02:30,083
On January 25th, 1951, Ridgway

63
00:02:30,103 --> 00:02:31,961
ordered his first counterattack,

64
00:02:31,981 --> 00:02:34,680
under the name Operation Thunderbolt.

65
00:02:34,855 --> 00:02:36,520
UN forces cautiously began

66
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:38,989
probing and pushing north again.

67
00:02:39,075 --> 00:02:41,173
One of the spearheads was the 23rd

68
00:02:41,193 --> 00:02:42,902
Infantry Regiment of the U.S.

69
00:02:42,922 --> 00:02:45,381
2nd Division, on the central front.

70
00:02:45,562 --> 00:02:48,528
Its commander was Colonel Paul Freeman.

71
00:02:48,684 --> 00:02:51,748
A calm, seasoned officer who had led this

72
00:02:51,768 --> 00:02:54,255
regiment since the Naktong River line.

73
00:02:54,452 --> 00:02:55,886
And attached to this regiment

74
00:02:55,906 --> 00:02:57,933
was one very special unit.

75
00:02:58,033 --> 00:03:00,370
The French Battalion of the United Nations,

76
00:03:00,390 --> 00:03:03,797
led by Lieutenant Colonel Ralph Monclar.

77
00:03:03,980 --> 00:03:06,362
Monclar had in fact been a three-star

78
00:03:06,382 --> 00:03:08,455
lieutenant general in the French Army.

79
00:03:08,545 --> 00:03:10,073
A fifty-nine-year-old veteran,

80
00:03:10,093 --> 00:03:11,916
he had voluntarily stepped down to

81
00:03:11,936 --> 00:03:13,749
lieutenant colonel so he could lead the

82
00:03:13,769 --> 00:03:16,570
battalion bound for Korea himself.

83
00:03:16,926 --> 00:03:19,776
In 1940, at Narvik in Norway,

84
00:03:19,796 --> 00:03:22,394
he had led a Foreign Legion half-brigade

85
00:03:22,414 --> 00:03:25,011
into battle, a legendary soldier.

86
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:26,844
Wounded again and again in two

87
00:03:26,864 --> 00:03:28,893
world wars, he had returned

88
00:03:28,913 --> 00:03:31,161
to the battlefield every time.

89
00:03:31,333 --> 00:03:33,009
Many of the men who followed him were

90
00:03:33,029 --> 00:03:35,922
Second World War veterans and volunteers.

91
00:03:36,032 --> 00:03:38,140
Tough men, unafraid of mountain

92
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:39,968
fighting or night combat.

93
00:03:40,158 --> 00:03:41,759
At first, the Americans were

94
00:03:41,779 --> 00:03:43,797
reportedly unsure what to make of

95
00:03:43,817 --> 00:03:45,856
this unfamiliar French unit.

96
00:03:45,905 --> 00:03:47,194
But that doubt would soon

97
00:03:47,214 --> 00:03:49,292
turn into deep respect.

98
00:03:49,624 --> 00:03:51,145
The story you are watching is the

99
00:03:51,165 --> 00:03:53,091
record of men who held out to

100
00:03:53,111 --> 00:03:55,811
the end inside an encircled ring.

101
00:03:55,937 --> 00:03:57,954
So that this record is never forgotten,

102
00:03:57,974 --> 00:04:01,222
please join us with a subscribe and a like.

103
00:04:01,553 --> 00:04:04,458
Then on January 29th, a patrol heading

104
00:04:04,478 --> 00:04:06,983
for the Twin Tunnels ran into an ambush.

105
00:04:07,187 --> 00:04:09,950
About five kilometers southeast of Chipyong-ni,

106
00:04:09,970 --> 00:04:11,783
there was a valley where the Central

107
00:04:11,803 --> 00:04:14,582
Line railway ran through two tunnels.

108
00:04:14,650 --> 00:04:15,847
The Americans called this

109
00:04:15,867 --> 00:04:17,918
place the Twin Tunnels.

110
00:04:18,041 --> 00:04:20,082
It overlooked the railway and the road to

111
00:04:20,102 --> 00:04:23,054
Wonju, a chokepoint among chokepoints.

112
00:04:23,239 --> 00:04:25,021
The patrol drove its trucks up near the

113
00:04:25,041 --> 00:04:27,491
tunnels and was pinned down by fire

114
00:04:27,511 --> 00:04:30,006
pouring in from the surrounding hills.

115
00:04:30,135 --> 00:04:32,152
After holding on for hours, clinging to

116
00:04:32,172 --> 00:04:34,994
the radio, only some of them were rescued.

117
00:04:35,176 --> 00:04:36,491
The Chinese had been hiding

118
00:04:36,511 --> 00:04:38,516
a large force in this area.

119
00:04:38,547 --> 00:04:40,541
It was the 125th Division

120
00:04:40,561 --> 00:04:42,834
of the Chinese 42nd Army.

121
00:04:43,027 --> 00:04:45,866
On January 31st, Freeman led the 3rd

122
00:04:45,886 --> 00:04:48,563
Battalion and the French Battalion in and

123
00:04:48,583 --> 00:04:51,192
seized the hills around the tunnels.

124
00:04:51,317 --> 00:04:53,335
That night, the men dug into

125
00:04:53,355 --> 00:04:55,134
the frozen ground and built

126
00:04:55,154 --> 00:04:57,749
positions facing every direction.

127
00:04:57,935 --> 00:05:00,207
Before dawn on February 1st,

128
00:05:00,227 --> 00:05:02,552
the Chinese surged forward to the

129
00:05:02,572 --> 00:05:04,725
sound of bugles and whistles.

130
00:05:04,891 --> 00:05:07,523
Several regiments of the 125th Division

131
00:05:07,543 --> 00:05:10,083
fell on the hills one after another.

132
00:05:10,144 --> 00:05:11,819
They meant to surround the Americans

133
00:05:11,839 --> 00:05:13,277
and French around the tunnels

134
00:05:13,297 --> 00:05:15,859
completely, front and rear.

135
00:05:16,030 --> 00:05:18,121
One French company position collapsed,

136
00:05:18,141 --> 00:05:19,634
and the enemy came right up

137
00:05:19,654 --> 00:05:21,716
to the battalion command post.

138
00:05:21,737 --> 00:05:23,971
As ammunition ran low, the French

139
00:05:23,991 --> 00:05:25,803
reportedly fixed bayonets and

140
00:05:25,823 --> 00:05:27,573
leapt out of their trenches.

141
00:05:27,769 --> 00:05:30,907
On the snowy slopes, men fought hand to hand,

142
00:05:30,927 --> 00:05:33,467
close enough to see each other's faces.

143
00:05:33,546 --> 00:05:35,528
The French Battalion retook its lost

144
00:05:35,548 --> 00:05:37,821
position and never gave up the hill.

145
00:05:38,009 --> 00:05:40,251
In the afternoon the clouds lifted,

146
00:05:40,271 --> 00:05:41,776
and fighter-bombers began

147
00:05:41,796 --> 00:05:44,245
pounding the Chinese on the hills.

148
00:05:44,302 --> 00:05:47,242
And by dusk, the Chinese withdrew,

149
00:05:47,262 --> 00:05:49,909
leaving countless bodies behind.

150
00:05:50,104 --> 00:05:51,923
The Americans recorded counting more

151
00:05:51,943 --> 00:05:54,348
than thirteen hundred Chinese bodies

152
00:05:54,368 --> 00:05:56,737
in front of their positions alone.

153
00:05:56,818 --> 00:05:59,324
They estimated total Chinese casualties

154
00:05:59,344 --> 00:06:01,577
at around thirty-six hundred.

155
00:06:01,628 --> 00:06:03,896
Their own losses came to about two hundred

156
00:06:03,916 --> 00:06:06,844
and fifty, including forty-five killed.

157
00:06:07,190 --> 00:06:08,747
It was the first time the Americans

158
00:06:08,767 --> 00:06:09,817
and French had stopped a

159
00:06:09,837 --> 00:06:12,161
major Chinese assault head-on.

160
00:06:12,222 --> 00:06:14,148
With new confidence, the 23rd

161
00:06:14,168 --> 00:06:16,174
Regiment headed for a small crossroads

162
00:06:16,194 --> 00:06:17,831
village to the northwest.

163
00:06:18,014 --> 00:06:20,197
On February 3rd, the regiment

164
00:06:20,217 --> 00:06:22,268
moved into Chipyong-ni.

165
00:06:22,395 --> 00:06:25,062
Roads to Yangpyeong, Yeoju and Wonju

166
00:06:25,082 --> 00:06:27,054
met here, and the Central Line

167
00:06:27,074 --> 00:06:29,705
railway had its Chipyong station.

168
00:06:29,811 --> 00:06:31,305
The regiment immediately began

169
00:06:31,325 --> 00:06:32,796
surveying the hills around the

170
00:06:32,816 --> 00:06:35,193
village and choosing its positions.

171
00:06:35,363 --> 00:06:37,153
Losing it would open the gateway to

172
00:06:37,173 --> 00:06:39,849
the entire central front to the enemy.

173
00:06:39,948 --> 00:06:42,227
For Ridgway, Chipyong-ni was a

174
00:06:42,247 --> 00:06:44,736
wedge he simply had to hold.

175
00:06:44,935 --> 00:06:47,320
But the terrain was the worst possible.

176
00:06:47,380 --> 00:06:49,932
The village sat in a basin barely a hundred

177
00:06:49,952 --> 00:06:52,106
meters above sea level, ringed by

178
00:06:52,126 --> 00:06:54,890
mountains three to four hundred meters high.

179
00:06:55,087 --> 00:06:58,663
Eight high hills looked down on Chipyong-ni.

180
00:06:58,700 --> 00:07:00,490
To hold every one of those ridgelines

181
00:07:00,510 --> 00:07:02,289
would have taken a defensive line

182
00:07:02,309 --> 00:07:05,019
some nineteen kilometers around.

183
00:07:05,212 --> 00:07:06,513
It was far too long for

184
00:07:06,533 --> 00:07:08,060
the troops Freeman had.

185
00:07:08,229 --> 00:07:09,859
So he gave up the high ground,

186
00:07:09,879 --> 00:07:11,737
and drew a small circle linking

187
00:07:11,757 --> 00:07:13,795
the low hills around the village.

188
00:07:13,998 --> 00:07:15,794
A circular perimeter roughly one

189
00:07:15,814 --> 00:07:17,717
and a half kilometers across.

190
00:07:17,863 --> 00:07:19,811
The 1st Battalion held the north,

191
00:07:19,831 --> 00:07:22,657
the 3rd the east, the 2nd the south,

192
00:07:22,677 --> 00:07:24,796
and the French Battalion the west.

193
00:07:24,979 --> 00:07:26,419
At the center of the circle sat

194
00:07:26,439 --> 00:07:28,569
the artillery and the reserve.

195
00:07:28,755 --> 00:07:32,263
105 and 155 millimeter howitzers,

196
00:07:32,283 --> 00:07:34,596
and even anti-aircraft guns,

197
00:07:34,616 --> 00:07:37,149
could swing their barrels in any direction.

198
00:07:37,347 --> 00:07:40,332
Above all, the anti-aircraft unit's quad

199
00:07:40,352 --> 00:07:43,018
machine-gun half-tracks could pour a staggering

200
00:07:43,038 --> 00:07:46,022
curtain of fire onto troops on the ground.

201
00:07:46,150 --> 00:07:47,985
The soldiers reportedly called that

202
00:07:48,005 --> 00:07:50,984
terrifying firepower the meat chopper.

203
00:07:51,163 --> 00:07:53,489
Wherever the line was breached, the reserve

204
00:07:53,509 --> 00:07:55,981
inside could reach it within minutes.

205
00:07:56,091 --> 00:07:57,892
The small, tight circle was,

206
00:07:57,912 --> 00:08:00,705
in itself, one great fortress.

207
00:08:00,902 --> 00:08:03,347
The men broke the frozen earth with pickaxes,

208
00:08:03,367 --> 00:08:06,112
digging foxholes and connecting trenches.

209
00:08:06,132 --> 00:08:08,401
They strung barbed wire, and laid

210
00:08:08,421 --> 00:08:10,438
trip flares in front of their positions

211
00:08:10,458 --> 00:08:12,395
that would light up at a touch.

212
00:08:12,523 --> 00:08:14,654
Trenches linked the positions,

213
00:08:14,674 --> 00:08:17,340
so ammunition and wounded could move between

214
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:19,562
them out of the enemy's line of fire.

215
00:08:19,764 --> 00:08:21,497
Meanwhile, the situation

216
00:08:21,517 --> 00:08:24,342
outside was deteriorating fast.

217
00:08:24,443 --> 00:08:26,108
On the night of February 11th,

218
00:08:26,128 --> 00:08:28,236
the Chinese launched what became known

219
00:08:28,256 --> 00:08:30,938
as their Fourth Phase Offensive.

220
00:08:31,125 --> 00:08:34,201
North of Hoengseong, the ROK 8th Division

221
00:08:34,221 --> 00:08:36,250
was badly mauled and collapsed,

222
00:08:36,270 --> 00:08:39,501
and UN forces were pushed back toward Wonju.

223
00:08:39,524 --> 00:08:41,667
The 23rd Regiment at Chipyong-ni was

224
00:08:41,687 --> 00:08:44,580
left jutting out ahead, all on its own.

225
00:08:44,697 --> 00:08:47,420
At Hoengseong alone, it was a disastrous

226
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:50,049
defeat in which thousands of South Korean and

227
00:08:50,069 --> 00:08:52,630
American troops were killed or captured.

228
00:08:52,813 --> 00:08:55,747
On February 13th, patrols and aerial

229
00:08:55,767 --> 00:08:57,910
observers reported Chinese troops

230
00:08:57,930 --> 00:09:00,049
converging from every direction.

231
00:09:00,167 --> 00:09:02,344
One by one, the roads into

232
00:09:02,364 --> 00:09:04,143
the village began to be cut.

233
00:09:04,331 --> 00:09:05,541
Freeman proposed pulling

234
00:09:05,561 --> 00:09:07,442
back south toward Yeoju.

235
00:09:07,502 --> 00:09:11,315
But Ridgway was adamant. Hold Chipyong-ni.

236
00:09:11,658 --> 00:09:13,732
Ridgway promised that even if the regiment

237
00:09:13,752 --> 00:09:16,873
was surrounded, he would send a relief force.

238
00:09:17,019 --> 00:09:19,024
Freeman told his men: we will

239
00:09:19,044 --> 00:09:21,149
stay here, and fight it out.

240
00:09:21,325 --> 00:09:24,327
When the sun went down, a strange silence

241
00:09:24,347 --> 00:09:27,139
settled over the mountains around the basin.

242
00:09:27,256 --> 00:09:29,752
Fifty-six hundred men held their breath in

243
00:09:29,772 --> 00:09:33,281
frozen foxholes, staring into the dark.

244
00:09:33,619 --> 00:09:35,153
And at ten o'clock on the night of

245
00:09:35,173 --> 00:09:38,917
February 13th, the first bugle sounded.

246
00:09:39,104 --> 00:09:41,672
The Chinese surged in from the north,

247
00:09:41,692 --> 00:09:44,528
the northwest and the southeast all at once.

248
00:09:44,705 --> 00:09:46,415
Soon the perimeter was under attack

249
00:09:46,435 --> 00:09:48,248
from every direction, a full

250
00:09:48,268 --> 00:09:50,885
three hundred and sixty degrees.

251
00:09:51,065 --> 00:09:52,912
Bugles, whistles and gongs

252
00:09:52,932 --> 00:09:54,743
tore through the darkness.

253
00:09:54,834 --> 00:09:58,023
When one line fell, another came on behind it,

254
00:09:58,043 --> 00:10:00,630
climbing over its fallen comrades.

255
00:10:00,720 --> 00:10:02,362
All night long, the Chinese

256
00:10:02,382 --> 00:10:04,399
probed here and there, searching

257
00:10:04,419 --> 00:10:06,438
for a weak spot in the line.

258
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:08,859
Each time a flare burst, Chinese

259
00:10:08,879 --> 00:10:10,282
soldiers in quilted cotton

260
00:10:10,302 --> 00:10:12,877
uniforms were exposed on the snow.

261
00:10:12,944 --> 00:10:14,972
Machine guns and mortars fired

262
00:10:14,992 --> 00:10:17,322
without pause into that white light.

263
00:10:17,505 --> 00:10:18,709
The guns at the center of the

264
00:10:18,729 --> 00:10:20,747
circle kept turning in every direction,

265
00:10:20,767 --> 00:10:23,080
firing round after round.

266
00:10:23,187 --> 00:10:25,398
Inside an encircled ring,

267
00:10:25,418 --> 00:10:27,560
whichever way you faced, the front

268
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:29,346
line was right in front of you.

269
00:10:29,539 --> 00:10:31,711
The most famous scene played out on the west

270
00:10:31,731 --> 00:10:34,544
side, in the French Battalion's sector.

271
00:10:34,664 --> 00:10:36,909
A mass of Chinese troops had pushed right

272
00:10:36,929 --> 00:10:39,043
up to the edge of the French trenches.

273
00:10:39,229 --> 00:10:41,673
At that moment, a piercing mechanical

274
00:10:41,693 --> 00:10:44,608
wail rose from the French lines.

275
00:10:44,777 --> 00:10:46,987
It was a hand-cranked siren.

276
00:10:47,182 --> 00:10:49,281
As the siren drowned out the bugles,

277
00:10:49,301 --> 00:10:51,045
the French fixed bayonets and

278
00:10:51,065 --> 00:10:53,013
charged out of their trenches.

279
00:10:53,121 --> 00:10:55,367
Hurling grenades and shouting, they plunged

280
00:10:55,387 --> 00:10:57,399
straight into the middle of the enemy.

281
00:10:57,594 --> 00:10:59,409
A sound no one expected,

282
00:10:59,429 --> 00:11:01,788
and bayonets coming straight at them.

283
00:11:01,865 --> 00:11:03,632
The Chinese ranks scattered,

284
00:11:03,652 --> 00:11:06,844
and the western sector held to the end.

285
00:11:07,026 --> 00:11:08,747
Bugles and gongs had been the

286
00:11:08,767 --> 00:11:10,602
enemy's weapons of terror.

287
00:11:10,710 --> 00:11:13,172
Monclar's men had, in effect,

288
00:11:13,192 --> 00:11:15,193
answered them with a siren.

289
00:11:15,542 --> 00:11:17,744
At dawn on February 14th,

290
00:11:17,764 --> 00:11:20,053
the Chinese fell back into the hills,

291
00:11:20,073 --> 00:11:22,319
leaving their dead behind.

292
00:11:22,407 --> 00:11:24,334
The first night had been survived.

293
00:11:24,354 --> 00:11:26,195
But it was only the beginning.

294
00:11:26,388 --> 00:11:28,314
That day, Colonel Freeman himself was

295
00:11:28,334 --> 00:11:31,444
wounded in the leg by mortar fragments.

296
00:11:31,540 --> 00:11:33,693
Even so, he limped around the perimeter

297
00:11:33,713 --> 00:11:35,806
and never stopped commanding.

298
00:11:35,979 --> 00:11:38,851
Ammunition and food were running out fast.

299
00:11:38,921 --> 00:11:40,848
When daylight came, transport

300
00:11:40,868 --> 00:11:43,238
planes flew over and dropped supplies

301
00:11:43,258 --> 00:11:45,575
by parachute into the circle.

302
00:11:45,761 --> 00:11:48,313
Some of the bundles landed outside the perimeter,

303
00:11:48,333 --> 00:11:50,885
inside the enemy's field of fire.

304
00:11:51,062 --> 00:11:52,225
Men who climbed out of their

305
00:11:52,245 --> 00:11:54,467
foxholes to retrieve them were

306
00:11:54,487 --> 00:11:56,580
sometimes cut down by snipers.

307
00:11:56,779 --> 00:11:58,695
Helicopters brought in ammunition

308
00:11:58,715 --> 00:12:00,904
and medical supplies, and flew the

309
00:12:00,924 --> 00:12:03,453
badly wounded out on the way back.

310
00:12:03,490 --> 00:12:05,462
They were the only thread linking the

311
00:12:05,482 --> 00:12:08,131
encircled ring to the outside world.

312
00:12:08,324 --> 00:12:10,884
The same day, to the southeast at Wonju,

313
00:12:10,904 --> 00:12:13,798
Chinese columns moving down along the Som River

314
00:12:13,818 --> 00:12:16,916
were caught in massed UN artillery fire.

315
00:12:16,995 --> 00:12:19,320
With this barrage, which the Americans

316
00:12:19,340 --> 00:12:22,156
called the Wonju Shoot, the Chinese were

317
00:12:22,176 --> 00:12:24,493
stopped on the Wonju front as well.

318
00:12:24,674 --> 00:12:26,538
On the night of February 14th,

319
00:12:26,558 --> 00:12:29,725
the Chinese came again in even greater numbers.

320
00:12:29,799 --> 00:12:32,420
Attacks that began around midnight hammered

321
00:12:32,440 --> 00:12:35,128
the north, the east and the west in turn.

322
00:12:35,238 --> 00:12:36,833
This time the attacks were bigger

323
00:12:36,853 --> 00:12:37,995
and more relentless,

324
00:12:38,015 --> 00:12:40,464
covered by mortars and machine guns.

325
00:12:40,659 --> 00:12:43,250
In the east, Company I of the 3rd

326
00:12:43,270 --> 00:12:45,571
Battalion was overrun for a time,

327
00:12:45,660 --> 00:12:47,553
but its men counterattacked and

328
00:12:47,573 --> 00:12:49,731
quickly took the position back.

329
00:12:50,066 --> 00:12:52,744
Sergeant First Class William Sitman of

330
00:12:52,764 --> 00:12:55,737
Company M threw himself on a grenade

331
00:12:55,757 --> 00:12:58,662
that landed in his machine-gun position.

332
00:12:58,839 --> 00:13:00,992
His sacrifice saved five of his

333
00:13:01,012 --> 00:13:03,372
comrades, and he was posthumously

334
00:13:03,392 --> 00:13:05,088
awarded the Medal of Honor.

335
00:13:05,281 --> 00:13:06,496
Then the assaults began to

336
00:13:06,516 --> 00:13:07,988
concentrate on the south,

337
00:13:08,008 --> 00:13:10,912
the sector held by the 2nd Battalion.

338
00:13:11,009 --> 00:13:13,139
Wave after wave of the enemy

339
00:13:13,159 --> 00:13:15,018
pressed against the foxholes of

340
00:13:15,038 --> 00:13:17,514
Company G on the southern hill.

341
00:13:17,717 --> 00:13:20,505
Private First Class Bruno Orig of Company

342
00:13:20,525 --> 00:13:23,225
G stayed alone on his machine gun to

343
00:13:23,245 --> 00:13:25,911
buy time for his comrades to pull back.

344
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:28,347
He was killed where he stood, and he

345
00:13:28,367 --> 00:13:30,665
too would receive the Medal of Honor.

346
00:13:31,011 --> 00:13:33,367
Before dawn on February 15th,

347
00:13:33,387 --> 00:13:36,320
the Company G position finally fell.

348
00:13:36,519 --> 00:13:38,144
The Chinese poured down from the

349
00:13:38,164 --> 00:13:41,218
direction of Hill 397 and drove into

350
00:13:41,238 --> 00:13:43,186
the southern side of the ring.

351
00:13:43,293 --> 00:13:46,051
If the gap could not be sealed, the whole

352
00:13:46,071 --> 00:13:48,715
perimeter might collapse from the inside.

353
00:13:48,909 --> 00:13:51,359
Freeman scraped together his last reserve,

354
00:13:51,379 --> 00:13:54,045
the Ranger company, along with men from other

355
00:13:54,065 --> 00:13:56,913
companies, and sent them to counterattack.

356
00:13:56,993 --> 00:13:58,771
But after hand-to-hand fighting on the

357
00:13:58,791 --> 00:14:01,548
snowy slope, the counterattack failed.

358
00:14:01,724 --> 00:14:03,718
Even after daybreak, the Chinese who had

359
00:14:03,738 --> 00:14:06,172
taken the southern hill would not budge.

360
00:14:06,512 --> 00:14:08,468
Ammunition was nearly gone, and the

361
00:14:08,488 --> 00:14:11,074
hole in the south could not be closed.

362
00:14:11,134 --> 00:14:12,742
The only hope left was a relief

363
00:14:12,762 --> 00:14:14,652
force coming up from the south.

364
00:14:14,856 --> 00:14:17,846
At that very hour, to the south, near Yeoju,

365
00:14:17,866 --> 00:14:20,157
the 5th Cavalry Regiment of the U.S.

366
00:14:20,177 --> 00:14:22,308
1st Cavalry Division was fighting

367
00:14:22,328 --> 00:14:25,141
its way north toward Chipyong-ni.

368
00:14:25,228 --> 00:14:26,414
It was the very relief

369
00:14:26,434 --> 00:14:28,213
force Ridgway had promised.

370
00:14:28,397 --> 00:14:30,015
But on foot, they could never reach

371
00:14:30,035 --> 00:14:32,656
Chipyong-ni before nightfall.

372
00:14:32,775 --> 00:14:34,486
The regimental commander, Colonel

373
00:14:34,506 --> 00:14:37,081
Marcel Crombez, decided to punch

374
00:14:37,101 --> 00:14:39,792
through the enemy with tanks alone.

375
00:14:39,995 --> 00:14:43,944
Thirteen M46 Patton tanks and ten

376
00:14:43,964 --> 00:14:47,313
M4 Shermans, twenty-three in all.

377
00:14:47,367 --> 00:14:49,362
And clinging to the tanks were a hundred

378
00:14:49,382 --> 00:14:52,264
and sixty infantrymen of Company L.

379
00:14:52,350 --> 00:14:54,094
The battalion commander, Lieutenant

380
00:14:54,114 --> 00:14:56,177
Colonel Treacy, objected to putting

381
00:14:56,197 --> 00:14:58,328
infantry on top of the tanks,

382
00:14:58,348 --> 00:15:01,378
but Crombez did not change his decision.

383
00:15:01,570 --> 00:15:03,555
At three forty-five in the afternoon of

384
00:15:03,575 --> 00:15:06,720
February 15th, the column entered the narrow

385
00:15:06,740 --> 00:15:09,779
valley road leading through Koksu-ri.

386
00:15:09,810 --> 00:15:11,053
About ten kilometers

387
00:15:11,073 --> 00:15:13,407
remained to Chipyong-ni.

388
00:15:13,613 --> 00:15:15,858
Machine guns, mortars and rockets rained

389
00:15:15,878 --> 00:15:18,304
down from the hills on both sides.

390
00:15:18,404 --> 00:15:20,627
The tanks kept racing on without stopping,

391
00:15:20,647 --> 00:15:22,983
and each time, infantrymen clinging

392
00:15:23,003 --> 00:15:25,691
to them were thrown off onto the road.

393
00:15:25,788 --> 00:15:28,283
Passing through Koksu-ri, Crombez's

394
00:15:28,303 --> 00:15:30,368
column was forced to halt twice.

395
00:15:30,542 --> 00:15:31,943
When the lead tank was hit and

396
00:15:31,963 --> 00:15:34,083
stopped, Crombez drove his own

397
00:15:34,103 --> 00:15:36,233
tank forward to lead the column.

398
00:15:36,417 --> 00:15:38,950
Stop, and everyone dies.

399
00:15:38,970 --> 00:15:40,906
That was his calculation.

400
00:15:41,069 --> 00:15:43,343
About a mile south of Chipyong-ni,

401
00:15:43,363 --> 00:15:46,074
in front of a small village called Masan,

402
00:15:46,174 --> 00:15:47,964
the tanks slammed head-on into

403
00:15:47,984 --> 00:15:49,797
the Chinese dug in on the hills

404
00:15:49,817 --> 00:15:51,942
along both sides of the road.

405
00:15:52,133 --> 00:15:54,783
Tank guns and machine guns swept the

406
00:15:54,803 --> 00:15:57,047
trenches on the hills one by one.

407
00:15:57,205 --> 00:15:59,427
Inside the perimeter, the 23rd Regiment

408
00:15:59,447 --> 00:16:01,646
attacked the southern hill again, and the

409
00:16:01,666 --> 00:16:04,259
Chinese were caught between front and rear.

410
00:16:04,447 --> 00:16:06,240
Around five in the afternoon,

411
00:16:06,260 --> 00:16:08,516
Crombez's tanks finally rolled

412
00:16:08,536 --> 00:16:10,644
into the Chipyong-ni perimeter.

413
00:16:10,742 --> 00:16:12,361
Inside the encircled ring,

414
00:16:12,381 --> 00:16:14,546
the men erupted in cheers.

415
00:16:14,670 --> 00:16:16,140
The relief tanks had struck the

416
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:18,154
Chinese holding the south side of the

417
00:16:18,174 --> 00:16:20,382
perimeter squarely from behind.

418
00:16:20,579 --> 00:16:22,095
But the price was high.

419
00:16:22,191 --> 00:16:24,196
Of the hundred and sixty infantrymen

420
00:16:24,216 --> 00:16:25,904
who had set out on the tanks,

421
00:16:25,924 --> 00:16:29,044
only about twenty-three reached Chipyong-ni.

422
00:16:29,394 --> 00:16:30,922
Many of the men left behind on

423
00:16:30,942 --> 00:16:33,141
the road were killed or captured.

424
00:16:33,218 --> 00:16:36,009
To this day, Crombez's decision is debated,

425
00:16:36,029 --> 00:16:37,773
somewhere between a brave

426
00:16:37,793 --> 00:16:40,253
breakthrough and a reckless sacrifice.

427
00:16:40,439 --> 00:16:42,158
That night, the Chinese lifted the

428
00:16:42,178 --> 00:16:45,083
encirclement and began withdrawing north.

429
00:16:45,163 --> 00:16:47,863
And so the three-day siege came to an end.

430
00:16:47,961 --> 00:16:50,434
Blocked at both Wonju and Chipyong-ni

431
00:16:50,454 --> 00:16:52,426
at once, the Chinese Fourth

432
00:16:52,446 --> 00:16:54,857
Phase Offensive lost its momentum.

433
00:16:55,050 --> 00:16:57,355
On the morning of February 16th,

434
00:16:57,375 --> 00:16:59,119
when the soldiers stepped out of their

435
00:16:59,139 --> 00:17:01,350
perimeter into the falling snow,

436
00:17:01,371 --> 00:17:03,457
abandoned weapons and equipment lay

437
00:17:03,477 --> 00:17:06,256
scattered endlessly across every slope.

438
00:17:06,354 --> 00:17:08,656
And among them, covered by snow,

439
00:17:08,676 --> 00:17:10,796
lay countless Chinese soldiers

440
00:17:10,816 --> 00:17:12,667
who would never return home.

441
00:17:13,014 --> 00:17:15,066
How did fifty-six hundred surrounded men

442
00:17:15,086 --> 00:17:17,944
hold off an enemy several times their size?

443
00:17:18,159 --> 00:17:20,724
Why the encircled ring survived.

444
00:17:20,813 --> 00:17:22,923
Let's break it down into four factors.

445
00:17:23,110 --> 00:17:23,613
First,

446
00:17:23,633 --> 00:17:26,019
the three-hundred-sixty-degree perimeter.

447
00:17:26,210 --> 00:17:28,515
Because the circle was small and tight,

448
00:17:28,535 --> 00:17:29,892
wherever it was breached,

449
00:17:29,912 --> 00:17:33,056
the reserve inside could rush there at once.

450
00:17:33,176 --> 00:17:34,544
Freeman's choice to give up the

451
00:17:34,564 --> 00:17:36,479
high ground was exactly what

452
00:17:36,499 --> 00:17:38,944
made the position so strong.

453
00:17:39,141 --> 00:17:42,161
Second, firepower and the sky.

454
00:17:42,361 --> 00:17:44,115
The artillery at the center of the ring

455
00:17:44,135 --> 00:17:46,971
poured out shells for three days straight,

456
00:17:46,991 --> 00:17:48,428
and whenever the weather cleared,

457
00:17:48,448 --> 00:17:50,808
fighter-bombers pounded the encirclement while

458
00:17:50,828 --> 00:17:53,953
transports and helicopters kept the ring alive.

459
00:17:54,167 --> 00:17:56,730
Third, the decision to hold.

460
00:17:56,922 --> 00:17:59,770
Ridgway chose to hold rather than retreat.

461
00:17:59,825 --> 00:18:01,580
And Freeman, with his wounded leg,

462
00:18:01,600 --> 00:18:04,221
and Monclar, at fifty-nine, fought

463
00:18:04,241 --> 00:18:06,679
beside their men to the very end.

464
00:18:06,891 --> 00:18:09,312
Fourth, the armored relief.

465
00:18:09,500 --> 00:18:11,507
Crombez's tanks tore through the

466
00:18:11,527 --> 00:18:13,396
encirclement without stopping.

467
00:18:13,516 --> 00:18:15,658
It came at a heavy cost, but that

468
00:18:15,678 --> 00:18:17,878
breakthrough saved the southern side

469
00:18:17,898 --> 00:18:20,100
just as it was about to collapse.

470
00:18:20,279 --> 00:18:23,252
According to U.S. records, UN forces at

471
00:18:23,272 --> 00:18:26,281
Chipyong-ni lost fifty-one killed,

472
00:18:26,301 --> 00:18:29,614
two hundred fifty wounded and forty-two missing.

473
00:18:29,710 --> 00:18:31,956
Chinese losses are estimated at

474
00:18:31,976 --> 00:18:34,426
around two thousand killed and three

475
00:18:34,446 --> 00:18:36,460
thousand wounded, though Chinese

476
00:18:36,480 --> 00:18:38,698
records give far lower figures.

477
00:18:39,027 --> 00:18:40,775
For this battle, the 23rd Regiment

478
00:18:40,795 --> 00:18:42,891
and the French Battalion received the U.S.

479
00:18:42,911 --> 00:18:44,883
Presidential Unit Citation.

480
00:18:44,991 --> 00:18:46,450
It was the moment the Americans'

481
00:18:46,470 --> 00:18:48,431
doubt about those unfamiliar French

482
00:18:48,451 --> 00:18:51,483
soldiers turned into deep respect.

483
00:18:51,667 --> 00:18:53,210
For the first time since entering

484
00:18:53,230 --> 00:18:55,225
the war, the Chinese had clearly

485
00:18:55,245 --> 00:18:57,386
backed down in a head-on fight.

486
00:18:57,447 --> 00:18:59,475
From this day on, the fear that

487
00:18:59,495 --> 00:19:01,240
had spread among UN troops

488
00:19:01,260 --> 00:19:03,508
began to turn into confidence.

489
00:19:03,683 --> 00:19:06,930
A week later, on February 21st, Ridgway went

490
00:19:06,950 --> 00:19:09,262
on the offensive with Operation Killer.

491
00:19:09,352 --> 00:19:10,720
And in mid-March,

492
00:19:10,740 --> 00:19:13,782
UN forces finally retook Seoul.

493
00:19:13,974 --> 00:19:15,806
That is why Chipyong-ni is called

494
00:19:15,826 --> 00:19:18,075
the Gettysburg of the Korean War.

495
00:19:18,109 --> 00:19:19,614
It was the turning point,

496
00:19:19,634 --> 00:19:22,003
where the tide of the war reversed.

497
00:19:22,152 --> 00:19:24,795
After Chipyong-ni, the Chinese never

498
00:19:24,815 --> 00:19:27,215
again pushed deep south of Seoul.

499
00:19:27,396 --> 00:19:30,316
Today, Chipyong-ni has a battle memorial

500
00:19:30,336 --> 00:19:33,218
hall and a monument to the French troops.

501
00:19:33,402 --> 00:19:36,194
Every year, ceremonies are held here to

502
00:19:36,214 --> 00:19:39,100
honor that victory and that sacrifice.

503
00:19:39,429 --> 00:19:41,162
Freeman later rose to the rank of

504
00:19:41,182 --> 00:19:43,767
four-star general in the U.S. Army.

505
00:19:43,817 --> 00:19:46,745
And Monclar passed away in 1964,

506
00:19:46,765 --> 00:19:48,470
at the age of seventy-two.

507
00:19:48,656 --> 00:19:50,712
One small circle, once drawn

508
00:19:50,732 --> 00:19:52,646
across a snow-covered basin.

509
00:19:52,715 --> 00:19:55,541
Today it lies quietly asleep among the rice

510
00:19:55,561 --> 00:19:58,704
paddies and fields of an ordinary village.

511
00:19:59,039 --> 00:20:01,670
Men who could have broken out and run.

512
00:20:01,737 --> 00:20:04,289
Instead they stayed where they were,

513
00:20:04,309 --> 00:20:05,685
and even with nothing at

514
00:20:05,705 --> 00:20:08,739
their backs, they did not retreat.

515
00:20:08,919 --> 00:20:10,465
If this story meant something to you,

516
00:20:10,485 --> 00:20:12,331
please subscribe and like.

517
00:20:12,437 --> 00:20:13,863
We will continue these fading

518
00:20:13,883 --> 00:20:16,150
records of war in the next story.
